  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The coastal path, known as the Tirepoil trail, lies at the southern tip of Cap d'Antibes. Its name is said to come from the sea wind that constantly ruffles visitors' hair. From the large car park on avenue André Sella, barely 200 metres of walking is enough to leave the built-up areas behind and face the Baie des Anges and Nice; all that remains is the sound of the sea, the wind and the gulls.</p>

      <h2>Good to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur Tourisme page, supplied by the Antibes Juan-les-Pins tourist office, lists the trail among places where animals are allowed; no specific leash rule is published. The site is protected: this part of the cape belongs to the Natura 2000 site "Baie et cap d'Antibes – îles de Lérins", managed by Posidonia Espace Mer et Littoral and the Conservatoire du littoral, and the tourist office reminds visitors that it must be respected. As a precaution, keep your dog on a leash, especially in narrow passages, and pick up after it.</p>
      <p>The tourist office gives about 5 km and 2 hours, moderate level. The route has paths and steps in the steep areas and remains easy as long as you wear comfortable shoes; it runs past the Château de la Garoupe (construction began in 1907), through a viewpoint from which you can sometimes spot whales and dolphins, offers a view of the Château de la Croë (1927), then passes Villa Eilenroc, built between 1860 and 1867 and bequeathed to the town of Antibes in 1982. The loop ends via avenue Mrs L. D. Beaumont and avenue de la Tour Gandolphe.</p>
      <p>Two things to note: the trail is closed in bad weather, rain or wind, and the stretch below Villa Eilenroc is closed until May 2027. The regional page states that the trail is not wheelchair accessible.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: avoid peak hours in summer — the trail is narrow in places and hard to pass in a group with a dog on a leash.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Garoupe beach is at the start of the trail. The tourist office also offers a guided tour of the trail, in French, to discover its species.</p>
    </div>

